                                                <div class="ssla_present_listing_right">
                                                    <div class="research_thrust_area_heading">
                                                        Hayavadana And Boiled Beans On Toast
                                                    </div>
                                                    <p class="m-0">
                                                        Directed and conceived by Dr. Suchetana Banerjee, both the plays by Girish Karnad introduced an interesting dichotomy in the tradition of SSLA Presents. For the first time, there was a parallel staging of two plays in two different languages, which also meant that they were to have completely different production processes. The production for Boiled Beans on Toast which was an English play involved a heavy usage and manipulation of quite a lot of props, to account for the multitude of scenes with different requirements. There was some homogeneity to this as most of the props were kept on stage to ease scene arrangements. Hayavadana which was originally in Kannada was staged in its Hindi translation. This play involved minimal use of props and had simple stage arrangements. Curtains on stage were manipulated on the most part to account for quite a lot of scene requirements. Both the plays involved the use of live music: while Hayavadana heavily relied on music, in order to get major plot points across, Boiled Beans on Toast on the other hand had a more minimal music set up with the play being concluded on a musical note by the entire ensemble.
                                                    </p>
                                                </div>
                                            </div>
                                            <div class="ssla_present_listing_right_mainbox">
