                                                <div class="ssla_present_listing_right">
                                                    <div class="research_thrust_area_heading">
                                                        Oedipus Rex And Medea
                                                    </div>
                                                    <p class="m-0">
                                                        For its third production, SSLA Presents went back to the roots of Western theatre and put up two Greek tragedies, Oedipus Rex by Sophocles and Medea by Euripides. Oedipus Rex was produced similar to how a Greek tragedy would look like while Medea was re-written with original musical elements. Professor Ananya Parikh states, “With Oedipus and Medea, the pleasure was to produce two plays simultaneously which would, in a way, challenge each other…Oedipus was supposed to be set in the past, trying to replicate what it would have been on a Greek stage more or less and Medea was of course a complete re-thinking… Medea becomes like this musical and Oedipus uses music but continues to hold onto the Greek traditions of what we know from readings of Aristotle about how those plays were done”.
                                                    </p>
                                                </div>
